Chapter: E1000UlpEntry
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Kernel chapter E1000e
  cites Kernel chapter Pci

 WHAT THIS CAN AND CANNOT PROVE, because the honest scope is the point.

 It CANNOT prove the write reaches the part. codex-vm models PHY page 770 and
 not page 779, which the `pch` stage's own neighbour control already reports
 (`ulp 779.16=ERR neighbour-id1=0154`), so no bed run can read this register
 back. That is also why the ULP row was promoted to a board reading rather
 than a bed pair.

 It CAN pin the ARITHMETIC against the citation, which is the half that a
 wrong constant would break silently. I219 rev 2.02 section 9.5.7.1 maps ULP
 Configuration 1 field by field: STICKY_ULP is bit 4, "Enter ULP on Link
 disconnect"; EN_ULP_LANPHYPC is bit 10, "Enable ULP on LAN disable
 (LANPHYPC)". Those two are the entry conditions and nothing else here is.

 THE BOARD'S OWN WORD IS THE FIXTURE. Sittings 8 and 9 both banked
 `ulp 779.16=0800`, so 0x0800 is used below as the value the mask must leave
 alone: bit 11 is inside the 13:11 the datasheet marks Reserved, and a mask
 that took it would be writing a reserved bit on a live part. The synthetic
 value adds both entry bits to that same word, so one row shows the mask
 clearing exactly what it should and keeping exactly what it should.

 We say:

Section: The Citation, As Arithmetic

  hex4 : Integer -> Text
  hex4 (v) = show v

  masked : Integer -> Integer
  masked (v) = bit-and v (bit-not e1000-ulp-entry-bits)

  board : Integer = #0800
  armed : Integer = #0C10

 THE GATE ROW USES A REAL MMIO BASE AND NOT ZERO, which matters for whoever
 flips the constant rather than for this run. With the gate off the step
 returns before touching anything, so a zero would be harmless today and would
 become a write to physical address 0x20 -- the IDT band -- the moment the
 constant went True. This chapter has already paid for bytes landing at
 address 0 once (`net-io-send-chunk`, 2026-07-14), and an arm that arms that
 trap for a future editor is not worth the two lines it saves.

  dev-id-of : PciScanResult -> Integer
  dev-id-of (scan) =
    when e1000-find scan
      is Just (d) -> (d.pci-device-id)
      is None -> 0

  mmio-of : PciScanResult -> Integer
  mmio-of (scan) =
    when e1000-find scan
      is Just (d) -> (e1000-init d).e-mmio
      is None -> 0

Section: The Pair

 THE PAIR RUNS FROM ONE BINARY AND THE KILL LEADS, which is red's standing
 condition and the reason it is built this way rather than as two images. The
 shipped constant is False, so `e1000-ulp-step` is the SKIPPING arm exactly as
 it ships: it must leave an armed register untouched. `e1000-ulp-disable` is
 the same write with no gate in front of it, so it is the REACHING arm. Same
 boot, same part, same register, and the only difference between the two rows
 is whether the gate was consulted.

 IT NEEDED A MODEL AND NOW HAS ONE. reek's `-i219-ulp-armed` (main 18837)
 powers 779.16 up with STICKY_ULP and EN_ULP_LANPHYPC SET on top of the
 measured 0x0800, so the write has something to clear.

 THAT THE FLAG IS LOAD-BEARING IS MEASURED, NOT ASSERTED. The same binary run
 against plain `-i219` answers 2048 on all three rows -- armed-before, after
 the gate, and after the write -- so without the flag this pair is VACUOUS and
 would pass while proving nothing. That is the decoy-arm failure L-FREEDOM
 names, and one run is what separates a pair that fires from a pair that
 cannot.

 The `after` row is the one that carries the citation: 0x0C10 becomes 0x0800,
 so both entry bits went and bit 11, inside the reserved 13:11, stayed.

  ulp-read : Integer -> Integer
  ulp-read (mmio) =
    let p = e1000-phy-set-page mmio e1000-ulp-page
    in let v = e1000-phy-read mmio e1000-ulp-reg
    in let restore = e1000-phy-set-page mmio 0
    in v

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console, Network.Read] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni ("sticky bit 4       : " & hex4 e1000-ulp-sticky)
    print-line-uni ("lanphypc bit 10    : " & hex4 e1000-ulp-lanphypc)
    print-line-uni ("entry bits         : " & hex4 e1000-ulp-entry-bits)
    print-line-uni ("board 0800 masked  : " & hex4 (masked board))
    print-line-uni ("armed 0C10 masked  : " & hex4 (masked armed))
    print-line-uni ("reserved bit kept  : " & (if bit-and (masked armed) #0800 /= 0 then "yes" else "no"))
    print-line-uni ("shipped ulp step   : " & (if e1000-pch-ulp-required then "on" else "off"))
    let scan = pci-scan-bus 0
    in let mmio = mmio-of scan
    in let did = dev-id-of scan
    in let armed-before = ulp-read mmio
    in let skipped = e1000-ulp-step mmio did
    in let after-skip = ulp-read mmio
    in let reached = e1000-ulp-disable mmio
    in let after-reach = ulp-read mmio
    in act
      print-line-uni ("gate answers       : " & show skipped)
      print-line-uni ("1 kill armed before: " & show armed-before)
      print-line-uni ("2 kill after gate  : " & show after-skip)
      print-line-uni ("3 pass after write : " & show after-reach)
      print-line-uni ("   write read back : " & show reached)
      print-line-uni ("   entry bits gone : " & (if bit-and after-reach e1000-ulp-entry-bits == 0 then "yes" else "no"))
      print-line-uni ("   reserved kept   : " & (if bit-and after-reach #0800 /= 0 then "yes" else "no"))
    end
  end
